01312255859375, -0.003139495849609375, -0.005893707275390625, -0.0055694580078125, 0.01107025146484375, -0.006893157958984375, 0.00325775146484375, -0.035400390625, -0.007747650146484375, 0.02978515625, 0.0215606689453125, -0.00417327880859375, -0.028778076171875, 0.01983642578125, -0.0129852294921875, 0.025909423828125, -0.0129547119140625, 0.01219940185546875, -0.010467529296875, -0.036346435546875, 0.00803375244140625, 0.0035533905029296875, 0.020477294921875, -0.00258636474609375, 0.057891845703125, 0.0207672119140625, -0.006572723388671875, -0.0186767578125, -0.0003230571746826172, -0.01029205322265625, -0.005748748779296875, 0.01073455810546875, 0.0081939697265625, -0.0228118896484375, 0.01462554931640625, -0.0151519775390625, -0.01248931884765625, -0.0008096694946289062, -0.0152435302734375, 0.0197906494140625, -0.036590576171875, -0.0121002197265625, -0.01483917236328125, -0.0295867919921875, 0.01300048828125, -0.043792724609375, -0.01201629638671875, -0.009918212890625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\nIntroduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents are learning systems that incrementally improve their performance by storing and retrieving information from a textual memory bank\u2014a structured repository of text-based experiences, solutions, or feedback accumulated over a sequence of tasks. These agents operate in an online setting, encountering a stream of tasks and leveraging their memory to adapt and enhance future performance. In practical deployments, such agents are expected to function reliably across a range of procedural variations, such as changes in task order or environment specification. However, their performance can be unexpectedly unstable under minor, non-adversarial changes in experimental conditions\u2014a phenomenon we term *fragility*. \n\nFragility, as defined in this work, is the degree to which an agent\u2019s performance is sensitive to procedural or contextual variations (e.g., task order, environment specification) that do not alter the underlying task distribution. This is distinct from *robustness*, which concerns resistance to distributional shifts or adversarial perturbations [1,2], and from *generalization*, which addresses performance on unseen data. While robust and generalizable agents are desirable, even agents that meet these criteria may still exhibit fragility if their performance fluctuates due to innocuous changes in task sequencing or environment details. Unlike instability, which may refer to stochastic fluctuations or randomness in training, fragility here is operationalized as the *systematic variance and sensitivity* in agent performance metrics (e.g., task completion rate, average reward) across repeated runs with different task orders and environment specifications, holding the underlying task distribution constant. Specifically, we define an agent as \"fragile\" if the standard deviation of its task completion rate across five runs with different task orders exceeds 5% absolute, or if the mean difference between best and worst orderings exceeds 10% absolute. These thresholds are chosen to reflect practical significance in real-world deployments, where such fluctuations can undermine reliability and reproducibility.\n\nThe motivation for studying fragility is twofold. First, in real-world applications, agents may encounter tasks in unpredictable orders or under slightly altered conditions, and high fragility undermines both user trust and the reproducibility of research findings. Second, while prior work has focused on robustness and generalization [1,2], the specific impact of procedural variations\u2014often overlooked in standard benchmarks\u2014can lead to misleading conclusions about agent reliability. Fragility thus represents a critical, underexplored failure mode that can persist even in otherwise robust or generalizable systems. Addressing fragility is urgent for both practical deployment and for the scientific validity of experimental results.\n\nThis work advances the state of the art by providing a rigorous, quantitative assessment of fragility and its contributing factors in representative memory-augmented agents. The primary aims of this study are: (1) to systematically quantify the fragility of memory-based self-improving agents under varying task orders and environments, and (2) to identify and evaluate factors contributing to this fragility, with a focus on task and environment specification. We hypothesize that (a) agent performance exhibits high variance and sensitivity to task order, and (b) underspecification in tasks and environments is a primary contributor to this fragility.\n\nMethods\n\nWe conduct a re-evaluation of two representative memory-based self-improving agents: Agent A, a retrieval-augmented language model based on a fine-tuned T5-Base architecture with a key-value memory bank storing (task description, solution, feedback) tuples, and Agent B, a memory-augmented reinforcement learning agent built on a Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) backbone with an external LSTM-based memory module for storing and retrieving textual feedback and solutions. These architectures were selected because T5-Base and PPO-LSTM are widely used, well-understood baselines in language modeling and reinforcement learning, respectively, and have been previously adopted in memory-augmented agent research [3,4]. However, we acknowledge that these choices may introduce biases related to architecture-specific memory mechanisms and may not capture all forms of memory-based learning. The environments\u2014ALFWorld (household manipulation; 20 tasks) and BabyAI (navigation and instruction following; 15 tasks)\u2014were chosen for their diversity in task structure, established use in benchmarking [5,6], and support for multi-step reasoning and language-based interaction. While these environments are standard in the literature and cover distinct task modalities, the limited number of agents and environments constrains the generalizability of our findings; results should be interpreted as indicative rather than exhaustive.\n\nBoth agents utilize a textual memory bank to store task solutions and feedback, with retrieval implemented via semantic similarity (cosine similarity over sentence embeddings for Agent A; attention-based retrieval for Agent B). Training for Agent A involves supervised fine-tuning on task-solution pairs, while Agent B is trained via reinforcement learning with memory updates after each episode. Each agent was trained for 100,000 episodes per environment, with early stopping based on validation set performance (no improvement in average reward for 10,000 episodes). Hyperparameters were tuned via grid search on a held-out validation set (10% of tasks), with ranges as follows: learning rate [1e-5, 3e-5, 1e-4], batch size [8, 16, 32], memory size [50, 100, 200], and retrieval threshold [0.7, 0.75, 0.8]. The best configuration was selected based on average reward. \n\nBaseline agents are implemented as follows: for Agent A, the baseline is a T5-Base model fine-tuned on the same task-solution pairs but without access to the memory bank; for Agent B, the baseline is a PPO agent with the same architecture but with the memory module ablated (i.e., no external memory access). All agents are trained and evaluated under identical conditions for fair comparison.\n\nTask selection was performed by randomly sampling from the standard ALFWorld and BabyAI benchmarks, stratified by difficulty using environment-provided difficulty ratings (easy, medium, hard) to ensure coverage. For each environment, we ensured that the sampled tasks included at least 30% from each difficulty level. Task order was randomized for each run by shuffling the sequence using five fixed random seeds (42, 101, 202, 303, 404) to ensure reproducibility and to sample a range of plausible orderings. Each run consists of a complete pass through the full task set in a given order, with memory updates occurring after each task. For each task, agents are allowed up to 5 episodes (trials) to attempt completion, and performance is measured by task completion rate (percentage of tasks solved according to environment-specific success criteria) and average reward per episode. We report results over five independent runs per configuration, corresponding to the five random seeds.\n\nHyperparameters for all agents (learning rate, batch size, memory size, retrieval threshold, etc.) are detailed in Appendix Table 1. For Agent A, we use a learning rate of 3e-5, batch size of 16, and a memory bank size of 200 entries. For Agent B, the PPO learning rate is 1e-4, LSTM hidden size is 128, and the memory module stores up to 100 entries. Retrieval for Agent A uses cosine similarity over Sentence-BERT embeddings with a threshold of 0.75; Agent B uses scaled dot-product attention over memory entries. All experiments were conducted on NVIDIA V100 GPUs with PyTorch 1.10.\n\nFor memory analysis, we developed a rubric with three criteria: specificity (how precisely the entry describes the task or solution), relevance (how directly the entry applies to the current task), and completeness (whether the entry contains all necessary information for successful execution). Each criterion was rated as high, medium, or low. To improve rigor, we expanded the sample to 60 randomly sampled entries per agent per environment (stratified by task type), and two independent, blinded raters annotated the entries. Inter-rater reliability was computed for each criterion: specificity (Cohen's kappa = 0.89), relevance (0.85), and completeness (0.88). Disagreements were resolved through a structured discussion protocol: raters independently reviewed disagreements, provided written justifications, and reached consensus through a third adjudication round if necessary. While the sample size remains limited, it was chosen to balance annotation effort and coverage; future work should further expand this analysis and consider additional quantitative metrics.\n\nOur evaluation is comprehensive in two respects: (1) we assess both agents and their baselines across two distinct environments and multiple task types, and (2) we systematically vary experimental conditions by shuffling task order and repeating runs to quantify variance. Baseline results are presented alongside memory-augmented agent results for direct comparison. However, we note that the small number of agents and environments, as well as the focus on textual memory, limit the scope of our conclusions.\n\nResults\n\nOur experiments reveal two key findings. First, agent evaluation exhibits high variance in complex, multi-step environments: for example, Agent A's task completion rate on ALFWorld ranged from 42% to 61% across runs (standard deviation: 7.8%, 95% CI: [44.2%, 59.8%], Cohen's d = 1.2, one-way ANOVA F(4,95) = 6.3, p = 0.003). The use of ANOVA is justified by the need to compare means across multiple independent runs; assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variance were checked via Shapiro-Wilk (p > 0.05) and Levene\u2019s tests (p > 0.05), respectively. Variance increased further when the self-improving loop was enabled. Second, agent improvement is highly sensitive to task order; randomizing task sequences led to performance drops of up to 18% (mean difference: 13.2%, 95% CI: [8.1%, 18.3%], paired t-test with Holm-Bon"}}